{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"a4609477f9661407","name":"card_unit_baltimore_mk_iii","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/Homefront/units/card_unit_baltimore_mk_iii","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"BALTIMORE Mk III","text":"Deployment: Deal 3 damage to an enemy unit if you deployed a British infantry last turn.","flavorText":"The Baltimore Mk III was a British-operated variant of the American-built Martin 187 Baltimore light bomber, employed extensively in the Mediterranean and Middle East during WWII. Built by the Glenn L. Martin Company, the Mk III incorporated several improvements over earlier versions, including upgraded Wright R-2600-19 engines and better defensive armament. This variant retained the twin .50 caliber machine guns in the dorsal turret and added enhanced armor and self-sealing fuel tanks for better survivability in combat. Deployed by the Royal Air Force thanks tor the Lend-Lease program, the Baltimore Mk III served in bomber and reconnaissance roles. It gained a reputation for ruggedness and reliability, particularly in desert operations. The RAF used it in tactical bombing missions over North Africa, Sicily, and Italy, often flying at low to medium altitudes to target enemy supply lines, airfields, and defensive positions. Despite initial concerns about visibility from the cockpit and handling characteristics, crews came to appreciate the Baltimore’s durability and payload capacity. The aircraft’s performance was particularly valued during the Tunisian campaign, where its speed and maneuverability made it difficult for Axis fighters to intercept. In total, 1,575 Baltimores were built, with a significant portion being Mk III variants.","faction":"Britain","type":"bomber","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":4,"operationCost":2,"attack":3,"defense":4},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_baltimore_mk_iii","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_baltimore_mk_iii_title","SourceString":"BALTIMORE Mk III","LocalizedString":"BALTIMORE Mk III"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::bomber","attack":3,"defense":4,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_baltimore_mk_iii_text","SourceString":"Deployment: Deal 3 damage to an enemy unit if you deployed a British infantry last turn.","LocalizedString":"Deployment: Deal 3 damage to an enemy unit if you deployed a British infantry last turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_baltimore_mk_iii_history","SourceString":"The Baltimore Mk III was a British-operated variant of the American-built Martin 187 Baltimore light bomber, employed extensively in the Mediterranean and Middle East during WWII. Built by the Glenn L. Martin Company, the Mk III incorporated several improvements over earlier versions, including upgraded Wright R-2600-19 engines and better defensive armament. This variant retained the twin .50 caliber machine guns in the dorsal turret and added enhanced armor and self-sealing fuel tanks for better survivability in combat. Deployed by the Royal Air Force thanks tor the Lend-Lease program, the Baltimore Mk III served in bomber and reconnaissance roles. It gained a reputation for ruggedness and reliability, particularly in desert operations. The RAF used it in tactical bombing missions over North Africa, Sicily, and Italy, often flying at low to medium altitudes to target enemy supply lines, airfields, and defensive positions. Despite initial concerns about visibility from the cockpit and handling characteristics, crews came to appreciate the Baltimore’s durability and payload capacity. The aircraft’s performance was particularly valued during the Tunisian campaign, where its speed and maneuverability made it difficult for Axis fighters to intercept. In total, 1,575 Baltimores were built, with a significant portion being Mk III variants.","LocalizedString":"The Baltimore Mk III was a British-operated variant of the American-built Martin 187 Baltimore light bomber, employed extensively in the Mediterranean and Middle East during WWII. Built by the Glenn L. Martin Company, the Mk III incorporated several improvements over earlier versions, including upgraded Wright R-2600-19 engines and better defensive armament. This variant retained the twin .50 caliber machine guns in the dorsal turret and added enhanced armor and self-sealing fuel tanks for better survivability in combat. Deployed by the Royal Air Force thanks tor the Lend-Lease program, the Baltimore Mk III served in bomber and reconnaissance roles. It gained a reputation for ruggedness and reliability, particularly in desert operations. The RAF used it in tactical bombing missions over North Africa, Sicily, and Italy, often flying at low to medium altitudes to target enemy supply lines, airfields, and defensive positions. Despite initial concerns about visibility from the cockpit and handling characteristics, crews came to appreciate the Baltimore’s durability and payload capacity. The aircraft’s performance was particularly valued during the Tunisian campaign, where its speed and maneuverability made it difficult for Axis fighters to intercept. In total, 1,575 Baltimores were built, with a significant portion being Mk III variants."},"operationCost":2,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"hasDeployment":true,"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/BALTIMORE_Mk_III.BALTIMORE_Mk_III","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":290.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_baltimore_mk_iii_photo","SourceString":"A RAF Martin Baltimore light bomber in flight in the Mediterranean Theater. Date unknown.","LocalizedString":"A RAF Martin Baltimore light bomber in flight in the Mediterranean Theater. Date unknown."},"blowUpExplosion":{"ObjectName":"Par